        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "The root स्वर्द् (svard) is ātmanepadi based on its anudātta marking in the Dhatupatha, grouped with स्वद् for the meaning \"to taste/relish.\" I'm now working out its desiderative form.\n\nAdding seṭ iṭ augment before the desiderative affix gives svardiṣ, and I'm checking reduplication: the abhyāsa for the initial cluster sv keeps the s since v isn't among the khay consonants, so the reduplicated syllable becomes si rather than following any special rule for v. I'm also verifying whether ṣatva should convert that s to ṣ — since this root's Dhatupatha listing uses dental s rather than ṣ (unlike स्वद् which is ṣopadeśa), the rule for ādeśa-based ṣatva doesn't apply here, so the form stays sisvardiṣa. I'm checking whether the rule restricting ṣatva to stu and ṇic-derived roots would block it anyway, confirming no ṣatva applies in this case.\n\nBuilding from there, I add ṇic to get sisvardiṣi, then form the causative of the desiderative as sisvardiṣayati, and with the prefix pra it becomes prasisvardiṣayati. For liṭ (perfect), since it's ṇijanta, I use the periphrastic construction with ām, and since this is kartari parasmaipada I settle on प्रसिस्वर्दिषयाञ्चक्रुः as the final answer, noting -āsuḥ and -babhūvuḥ as alternative periphrastic auxiliaries.\n\n",
